Program:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class TicketDiscount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter age: ");
        int age = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.print("Is it a round-trip? (yes/no): ");
        String tripType = sc.next();
        double discount = 0;
        if (age < 12) {
            discount = 50;
        } else if (age >= 60) {
            discount = 30;
        }
        if (tripType.equalsIgnoreCase("yes")) {
            discount += 10;
        }
        System.out.println("Final Discount: " + discount + "%");
    }
}
Explanation of Logic:
Branching (if-else) is used to determine the age-based discount.
A second independent if adds a 10% discount for round-trips.
This demonstrates clear separation of conditions and compound logic using decision-making constructs.
It also reflects real-world rule-based programming, where multiple conditions can independently affect an outcome.
